# Alan 9/12/26 - Retention is now indefinite. It used to be `rotate 14` with
# `maxage 30`, and `rotate 14` was what actually bound: every stem kept exactly
# 14 rotations plus the live file, which worked out to 13 days, not the 30 the
# maxage implied (`maxsize 25M` makes a busy day rotate twice and burn two
# slots). Thirteen days of ALL of these logs was 3.2 MB compressed against a
# 5.3 GB var/jobs, so the retention was buying nothing and costing history --
# errors.log alone is 2.8 KB/day, about 1 MB/year.
#
# Nothing is deleted now: `rotate` is set high enough to never be reached and
# `maxage` is gone. Revisit only if var/logs stops being a rounding error next
# to var/jobs.
/var/www/dikarya/var/logs/access.log /var/www/dikarya/var/logs/error.log /var/www/dikarya/var/logs/errors.log /var/www/dikarya/var/logs/worker.log /var/www/dikarya/var/logs/worker-bulk.log /var/www/dikarya/var/logs/scanner.log {
    daily
    maxsize 25M
    rotate 99999
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y%m%d-%H%M%S
    copytruncate
}

# Alan 8/15/26 - The metrics collector appends one JSON line per minute to this
# file and nothing ever truncated it, so it had grown to 36 MB since December and
# would keep going at roughly 50 MB a year. Nothing reads it back -- the
# monitoring dashboard calls collect_system_metrics() live -- so it is a pure
# write-only sink that only ever cost disk.
#
# Alan 9/12/26 - This one keeps a bounded window deliberately, unlike the logs
# above: it is machine telemetry nothing reads back, not a record of what
# happened, so age-limiting it loses nothing an operator would want.
/var/www/dikarya/var/metrics/system_metrics.jsonl {
    daily
    maxsize 25M
    rotate 7
    maxage 30
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y%m%d-%H%M%S
    copytruncate
}
